===Etymology 1===
{{rfe|lang=en|Any relation to German {{m|de|feucht||damp, dank}}?}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A heavy, musty, and unpleasant [[atmosphere]], usually in a poorly-ventilated area.
#* '''1996''', {{w|Janette Turner Hospital}}, ''Oyster'', Virago Press, paperback edition, page 4
#*: On certain days, when hot currents shimmered off Oyster's Reef, we would detect the chalk-dust of the mullock heaps, acrid; or, from the opal mines themselves, the ghastly '''fug''' of the tunnels and shafts.
#* '''2004''', {{w|John Derbyshire}}, "Boxing Day", ''National Review'', November 8, 2004
#*: The gym teacher left that year, his successors had no interest in boxing, and society soon passed into a zone where the idea of thirteen-year-old boys punching each other's faces for educational purposes became as unthinkable as the dense '''fug''' of tobacco smoke in our school's staff room.
#* '''2005''', {{w|J.K. Rowling}}, ''{{w|Harry Potter and the Half-blood Prince}}'', Bloomsbury, hardback edition, page 42
#*: The misty '''fug''' his breath had left on the window sparkled in the orange glare of the streetlamp outside.
#* {{quote-book|year=2008 |title=Going Postal |author=Terry Pratchett |page=288 |ISBN=1407035401 |passage= That's what a '''fug''' was. You could have cut cubes out of the air and sold it for cheap building material. }}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A state of [[lethargy]] and [[confusion]]; [[daze]].
#* {{quote-book|year=2011  |title=The Spoilt City: The Balkan Trilogy 2 |author=Olivia Manning |page= |ISBN=1446494578 |passage=So delicious after the '''fug''' of summer. It makes one feel so alive. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2015 |title=The Girl in the Photograph |author=Kate Riordan |page= |ISBN=1405917431 |passage=Somewhere in the '''fug''' of her mind she remembered how to close it and fetched the pole, slotting it into the mechanism above and beginning to turn the handles. }}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A state of [[chaos]] or [[confusion]].
#* {{quote-journal|year=2002|work=The Year's Best Science Fiction: Nineteenth Annual Collection|isbn=1429903821|title=Marcher|author=Chris Beckett|passage=There was a '''fug''' of fear in the room.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2006  |title=The Forging of Races |author=Colin Kidd  |page= |ISBN=1139457535 |passage=Viewed from this perspective, the Victorian era reeks of a suffocating and bigoted complacency, and, no doubt, many white imperialists existed in a '''fug''' of self-righteous superiority. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2013 |title=Bridget Jones: Mad about the Boy |author=Helen Fielding  |page=7 |ISBN=0224098098 |passage=But now am in total '''fug''' about what to text Roxster about tonight, and whether I should tell him about the nits. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2014 |title=The Cold of May Day Monday: An Approach to Irish Literary History |author=Robert Anthony Welch  |page= |ISBN=0191510467 |passage=Her translations are dimmed over with a '''fug''' of late eighteenthcentury poetic diction, a striving for sublimity or for sentimental effect. }}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|fugg}}

# To create a fug (heavy unpleasant atmosphere).
#* {{quote-book|year=2008 |title=The Swap |author=Antony Moore  |page=231 |ISBN=009950782X |passage=Inside, the Golden Lion was '''fugged''' with the smoke of too many cigarettes and the unhappy sound of a darts team practising. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2012 |title=The Heresy of Dr Dee |author=Phil Rickman |page= |ISBN=184887278X |passage=I'd walked down, for maybe the last time, from my lodgings behind New Fish Street, through air already '''fugged''' with smoke from the morning fires. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2013 |title=The Glass Republic: The Skyscraper Throne |author=Tom Pollock |page= |ISBN=1780870124 |passage=The rich sewer gases '''fugged''' around her and she shook her head, trying to clear it. }}
# To be surrounded by a fug (heavy unpleasant atmosphere).
#* {{quote-book|year=1921 |title=Everybody's Magazine - Volume 44 |author= |page=38 |passage="Well, I like it a jolly sight better than '''fugging''' up in those carriages with all that gassing crowd of Garden Home fussers." }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2005 |title=Light |author=Craig Taylor  |page=74 |ISBN=1905315007 |passage=The air was warm and close and the late afternoon sun was '''fugging''' through grey clouds and making them light - still grey, but light, really light. }}
# To put into a fug (state of lethargy and confusion).
#* {{quote-book|year=2011  |title=How Not to Grow Up!: A Coming of Age Memoir. Sort Of. |author=Richard Herring |page=34-35 |ISBN=0091932092 |passage=The adrenalin, though diminished, was still running through my veins; the red mist was lifting but my mind was '''fugged''' by this unfamiliar combination of hormones, slowly intermingling with indignity and contrition and the dawning of familiar, ignominious defeat. }}


